---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import '../styles/role-page.css';
---
<Layout
  title="Cytidel for CISOs · Cytidel"
  description="Your team gets the speed and the coverage to act on what matters. You get the evidence and the language to defend every decision — to your board, your auditors, and your regulators."
>
  <div class="role-page">
    <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-h1">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="hero-inner">
          <span class="eyebrow">Solution Guide</span>
          <h1 id="hero-h1">Cytidel for <span class="role">CISOs</span></h1>
          <p class="hero-sub">Your team gets the speed and the coverage to act on what matters. You get the evidence and the language to defend every decision — to your board, your auditors, and your regulators.</p>
          <div class="hero-cta">
            <a href="/book-a-demo" class="btn btn-primary" data-analytics="CTA|Book a Demo|Hero">
              Book a Demo
              <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pains" aria-labelledby="pains-h2">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-head">
          <h2 id="pains-h2">What's getting in your way</h2>
          <p class="lead">Three problems every CISO is sitting with right now.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pain-grid">
          <article class="pain">
            <span class="pain-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h10"/></svg></span>
            <h3>Your team is producing the right answers to the wrong questions.</h3>
            <p>The vulnerability management programme is patching what it can. The threat intelligence team is producing strong analysis. The supplier risk function is checking the boxes. And yet — when the board asks "are we exposed to the threat actor in the news this week?", the answer is a four-day research project, not a structured response.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="pain">
            <span class="pain-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M3 3v18h18"/><path d="M7 14l3-3 3 3 4-5"/></svg></span>
            <h3>The board is at the table now, and they're asking sharper questions.</h3>
            <p>Every breach in the news, every supplier failure, every regulatory shift puts the question back on them: are we at risk? You're being asked to translate technical exposure into business risk — every quarter, sometimes every month, occasionally in real time during an incident. CVSS doesn't translate. CVE IDs don't translate. You need a different language.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="pain">
            <span class="pain-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 3l8 3v5c0 5-3.5 8-8 10-4.5-2-8-5-8-10V6z"/></svg></span>
            <h3>Your regulators don't accept "we patched the criticals" anymore.</h3>
            <p>NIS2 and DORA both made supplier and software monitoring a continuous obligation. ISO 27001 auditors want evidence behind every prioritisation decision. The bar has moved from "do you have a policy" to "show us how the policy is enforced in real time." Your team can't manually generate that evidence trail every quarter.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="capabilities" aria-labelledby="cap-h2">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-head">
          <h2 id="cap-h2">What Cytidel does for CISOs</h2>
          <p class="lead">A platform that turns raw threat and vulnerability data into the language the board, the auditor, and the regulator actually need.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="capabilities-list">
          <div class="cap-item">
            <span class="cap-no">01</span>
            <div><h3>Real-time Vulnerability Intelligence</h3><p>gives your team a threat-led prioritisation engine — and gives you a real-time picture of what your organisation is exposed to, refreshed continuously. The board's question stops being a research project.</p></div>
          </div>
          <div class="cap-item">
            <span class="cap-no">02</span>
            <div><h3>Threat Actor Intelligence</h3><p>translates threat exposure into the language that matters. Not "we have 14,000 high-severity CVEs" — instead, "these are the threat actors targeting our industry, here's what they're using, and here's where we stand." Cytidel does the translation. You bring it to the board.</p></div>
          </div>
          <div class="cap-item">
            <span class="cap-no">03</span>
            <div><h3>Smart Alerts</h3><p>ensures the right intelligence reaches the right people, every time. Your team isn't checking dashboards out of anxiety — the signal comes to them when it matters. Your governance posture becomes evidenceable, not aspirational.</p></div>
          </div>
          <div class="cap-item">
            <span class="cap-no">04</span>
            <div><h3>Bulk Analyser</h3><p>turns the audit conversation. When an ISO 27001 or NIS2 auditor asks how you prioritise, you show them the methodology, the evidence, and the data trail behind every decision. Not a score and a gut feeling.</p></div>
          </div>
          <div class="cap-item">
            <span class="cap-no">05</span>
            <div><h3>Supplier &amp; Software Monitoring</h3><p>makes third-party risk a continuous discipline, not an annual exercise. NIS2 and DORA both require it. Cytidel delivers it.</p></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="scenarios" aria-labelledby="scen-h2">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="scenario-card">
          <div class="section-head">
            <h2 id="scen-h2">How CISOs use Cytidel</h2>
            <p class="lead">Three moments where the conversation changes.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="scenario-list">
            <article class="scenario">
              <div class="scenario-num">01</div>
              <div>
                <h3>The board question, mid-meeting</h3>
                <p>A board member asks about a ransomware group in the news. You pull the actor's profile from Threat Actor Intelligence on your phone — see the vulnerabilities in their arsenal, see what's in your stack, see where your team stands on remediation. You answer in the meeting, with data. The conversation moves to strategy, not panic.</p>
              </div>
            </article>
            <article class="scenario">
              <div class="scenario-num">02</div>
              <div>
                <h3>The quarterly board report</h3>
                <p>Instead of a slide deck full of CVE counts and CVSS distributions, you bring a report that says "these are the threat actors active in our industry this quarter, here's our exposure to each, here's what's been remediated, and here's our forward-looking risk." The board engages on risk, not on remediation theatre.</p>
              </div>
            </article>
            <article class="scenario">
              <div class="scenario-num">03</div>
              <div>
                <h3>The audit conversation</h3>
                <p>The NIS2 or ISO 27001 auditor asks how vulnerability management decisions are made. You walk them through Cytidel's evidence trail — exploitation status, threat actor association, real-time signal — and the audit shifts from "explain your gaps" to "explain your methodology." You're demonstrating a system, not defending a queue.</p>
              </div>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="conversation" aria-labelledby="conv-h2">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-head">
          <h2 id="conv-h2">The conversation it changes</h2>
          <p class="lead">The board wants to know which threats are real. Cytidel tells you, in their language.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="conversation-grid">
          <div class="conv">
            <span class="conv-label">The old conversation</span>
            <p>"We're patching to policy. CVSS criticals are being addressed. We have an annual vendor review process."</p>
            <div class="conv-verdict"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/><path d="M15 9l-6 6M9 9l6 6"/></svg>Doesn't answer the question the board is asking.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="conv is-new">
            <span class="conv-label">The new conversation</span>
            <p>"Three threat actors are active in our industry. We have exposure to two — both remediated, with the evidence trail. The third doesn't affect our stack. Our supplier monitoring flagged a vendor advisory yesterday; we've reached out for confirmation. Here's what we're watching for next quarter."</p>
            <div class="conv-verdict"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/><path d="M8 12l3 3 5-6"/></svg>The answer the board is actually asking for.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="conversation-close">Same team. Same posture. Same regulatory obligations. Completely different conversation with the people who matter most.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="final-cta" aria-labelledby="final-cta-h2">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="final-cta-card">
          <h2 id="final-cta-h2">Ready to see Cytidel?</h2>
          <p>Meet with a Cytidel expert to discuss how you can get ahead of emerging threats, understand the latest threat actor activity relevant to your business, and monitor critical software and suppliers 24/7.</p>
          <a href="/book-a-demo" class="btn btn-primary" data-analytics="CTA|Book a Demo|Final CTA">
            Book a Demo
            <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
